WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:22.850
Hello and welcome.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:25.930
Very good to be with you
and have your company.

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:28.860
How dangerous can fiction really be?

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:32.240
My guest today, a Turkish
writer and political scientist

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:35.500
has been investigated by the
authorities of her home country

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:37.795
for simply writing novels,

00:00:37.795 --> 00:00:39.410
accused of being anti-Turkish

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:41.530
and inciting crimes through her writing.

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:45.280
She has fought back and
continued to defy her critics.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.080
Elif Shafak, through her fiction,

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:50.220
including her latest novel,
"The Island of Missing Trees,"

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:53.731
covers politics, identity and
the expectations of women.

00:00:53.731 --> 00:00:58.696
She has also won acclaim for
defining the 40 rules of love.

00:00:58.696 --> 00:00:59.860
Now a British citizen,

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:02.310
she continues to campaign
for women's rights,

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:04.850
both in what she calls
her motherland, Turkey,

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:06.410
and around the world.

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:07.750
Elif Shafak, welcome.

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:08.750
Thank you so much.

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:10.110
It's lovely to be with you.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:11.100
I thought we could start

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.880
with what you call your
motherland, Turkey.

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:17.370
And do you think that people
outside of Turkey realize

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:21.260
how challenging the
circumstances are inside it?

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:23.620
I don't think many people
realize that Turkey,

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:25.910
as you know, has become a difficult place

00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:28.320
for anyone who deals with words,

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.350
so, poets, writers,
journalists, of course,

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:33.340
but also cartoonists for instance.

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:36.120
Humor itself can also
be regarded as dangerous

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:37.890
when democracy is lost.

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:40.010
So, that makes it very difficult

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:42.170
when you don't have freedom of speech.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:44.860
It makes the life of a writer difficult.

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:46.960
Many people, of course, understand

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:51.430
that it might be not that easy
to question political issues,

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:52.790
but sometimes people don't realize

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:55.950
that it's equally challenging
to write about sexuality,

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:59.020
gender discrimination, child abuse.

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:00.420
So, all those issues

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:03.770
can also be very difficult
subjects to tackle.

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:07.160
I want to come to the investigation
of you in short order,

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:10.180
but on that point of our view of Turkey,

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:13.380
outside Turkey, do you think it's outdated

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:14.700
what we think of as Turkey

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:17.269
because what's happened
has been quite gradual?

00:02:17.269 --> 00:02:19.230
There's no doubt in my mind,

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:20.980
this is such a beautiful country.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:23.380
It's a very complex country

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:27.060
and I would never associate
the government and the people.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.050
The civil society in Turkey is, of course,

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:32.250
far more multi-layered, nuanced,

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:35.110
but we do have a vague
conservative government

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:37.820
that has become more and more
authoritarian over the years.

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:38.653
Under Erdogan?

00:02:38.653 --> 00:02:39.486
Under Erdogan.

00:02:39.486 --> 00:02:40.530
So, what we've seen in Turkey

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:43.750
is the country under Erdogan
has been going backwards

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:48.050
and declining into
ultra-nationalism, Islamism,

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:49.890
populist authoritarianism.

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:51.460
When and where that happens,

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:53.600
we will also see an increase in sexism,

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:55.400
misogyny, homophobia.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:58.310
In my opinion, these things
always go hand in hand.

00:02:58.310 --> 00:02:59.480
That must be many though,

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:02.960
who are in support or
do you see it as a mask?

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:04.992
There are some in support, of course,

00:03:04.992 --> 00:03:07.960
but for all kinds of reasons.

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:09.070
And as you pointed out,

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:11.540
this government has been in
power for a very long time.

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:14.920
They came to power with
liberal promises of reform.

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:18.070
At the time they were pro
Turkey's EU membership.

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:19.960
Do you consider Turkey, I say,

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:21.160
used to call it your motherland,

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.180
but do you consider it your home?

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:25.890
What is your identity as
far as you're concerned,

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:26.723
because you're someone

00:03:26.723 --> 00:03:28.370
who's paid a lot of attention to that?

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:30.450
I care about this question so much.

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:34.820
Identity for me, it's not a
static, solid, single thread.

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:36.600
It's not a monolithic block.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:40.630
I want to think of identity
as more fluid, more multiple.

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:41.870
And I think as human beings,

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:45.400
we all contain multitudes
like Walt Whitman used to say.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:46.950
The problem is we're living in a world

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:48.930
that doesn't allow us to be multiple,

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:51.960
let alone celebrate our own multiplicity.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:55.670
So, for me, rather than
identity, singular,

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:58.600
what I care about more
is multiple belongings.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:00.670
If I have a singular identity,

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:02.250
if you have a singular identity,

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:04.080
it's much harder for us

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.010
to understand what we have in common.

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:07.970
But the moment we understand

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:10.920
and embrace these concentric
circles of belongings,

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:14.550
then we realize actually
so much of it overlaps.

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:17.737
You said of your own country,
your motherland in 2014,

00:04:17.737 --> 00:04:20.090
"I get a lot of criticism
from the cultural elite

00:04:20.090 --> 00:04:21.800
and a lot of love from readers.

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:23.740
The more you are read
in the Western world,

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:26.620
the more you are hated
in your motherland."

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:28.010
Do you think that's still the case?

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:30.620
Has it got worse or got better
in the intervening years?

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:32.430
I wish I could say that.

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:34.180
We made a lot of progress in Turkey

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:35.670
in terms of freedom of speech,

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:37.930
but I'm afraid it's quite the opposite.

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:41.240
In 2006, I was put on
trial for writing a novel

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:43.140
called "The Bastard of Istanbul,"

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:45.070
which tells the story of a Turkish family

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:47.220
and an Armenian American family,

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:50.010
and it deals with memory and amnesia.

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:52.950
As you know, in Turkey, we
have a very long history,

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:56.420
rich history, but that doesn't
translate into strong memory.

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:58.030
In fact, I think it's quite the opposite.

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:00.280
We're a society of collective amnesia

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:02.350
and our entire relationship with the past

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:03.970
is full of ruptures.

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:06.940
And that void is filled
in by ultranationalists

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:09.810
and Islamist interpretations of the past.

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:12.520
So, when this book came
out, the novel came out,

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:16.070
I was put on trial for
insulting Turkishness

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:17.840
under Article 301.

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:20.340
This is an article in
the Turkish constitution,

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:23.260
which has been used against
journalists, against scholars,

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:24.790
but it was the first time it was used

00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:26.550
against the work of fiction.

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:27.980
And it was quite surreal as well

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:30.930
because the words of fictional
characters were plucked.

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:33.520
They were taken out of the text and used

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:35.290
as evidence in the courtroom,

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:37.400
as a result of which my Turkish lawyer

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:40.270
had to defend my Armenian
fictional characters

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:41.103
in the courtroom.

00:05:41.103 --> 00:05:42.380
So, your characters were on trial.

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:44.670
My characters were on
trial alongside myself.

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:46.210
Because you fought this.

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:47.510
And what happened?

00:05:47.510 --> 00:05:49.790
So, that madness went on for about a year

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:51.480
and there were groups on the streets,

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:53.590
ultranationalists spitting at my pictures,

00:05:53.590 --> 00:05:56.180
burning EU flags, because for them,

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:58.040
anyone who questions official history

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:00.190
is a pawn of Western powers.

00:06:00.190 --> 00:06:02.460
And then I was acquitted
at the end of that.

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:04.410
What hold did that have on you,

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:06.490
because that sounds like it was something

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:08.450
to really live through?

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:09.870
It was quite unsettling.

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:12.840
I mean, I have to be honest to myself,

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:14.620
it did shake me quite a bit,

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:16.510
also because I was pregnant at the time.

00:06:16.510 --> 00:06:20.483
So, the day I was acquitted,
the next day I gave birth.

00:06:20.483 --> 00:06:21.316
Wow.

00:06:21.316 --> 00:06:24.470
So, just one day apart, the
birth and the acquittal.

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:28.310
So, that whole year was
quite unsettling for me

00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:30.350
and it was not that easy.

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:32.760
But of course, many people
go through so much worse.

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:35.680
So, I don't want to put
too much emphasis on that.

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:36.650
And at the same time,

00:06:36.650 --> 00:06:39.280
I did receive amazingly
heartwarming letters

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:41.509
at the time from readers.

00:06:41.509 --> 00:06:45.990
And the reason why I mention
where we are right now is,

00:06:45.990 --> 00:06:48.718
a couple of years ago in 2019, again,

00:06:48.718 --> 00:06:53.130
this time I was investigated
for crime of obscenity

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:56.740
for writing about sexual
harassment for gender violence.

00:06:56.740 --> 00:06:58.130
I write about these issues

00:06:58.130 --> 00:07:01.130
because this is the reality
of the land where I come from.

00:07:01.130 --> 00:07:03.560
And what happened with
those investigations?

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:05.450
It did not turn into a trial.

00:07:05.450 --> 00:07:07.500
But the very fact that police officers

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:08.860
come to a publishing house,

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:11.430
the very fact that they confiscate novels,

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:14.400
works of fiction and
they take to a prosecutor

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:16.740
is quite unsettling for authors.

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:18.540
Your works are a hugely successful

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:19.940
and widely read around the world,

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:21.500
that when you started writing in English,

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:23.100
I know that was a big deal for you.

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:25.670
But again, you've been labeled
a traitor for doing so,

00:07:25.670 --> 00:07:28.885
at making your name easier
for Westerners to pronounce

00:07:28.885 --> 00:07:29.718
and all of that.

00:07:29.718 --> 00:07:32.180
But how much do you think
you've been targeted

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:34.100
because you're also a woman?

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:36.720
It's difficult to be a novelist in Turkey

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:40.730
because novelists deal
with issues about identity,

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:43.070
belonging, memory, amnesia.

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:45.500
The canvas of a novel is so nuanced.

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:46.950
It's not easy to be a novelist.

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:49.410
But honestly, I think it's much harder

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:53.200
or it's perhaps more difficult
to be a woman novelist,

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:54.330
because additionally,

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:57.700
you have to deal with
sexism and misogyny as well.

00:07:57.700 --> 00:08:00.785
What is going on in Turkey
with regards to women

00:08:00.785 --> 00:08:05.100
is extraordinarily bad
on many, many levels.

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:08.101
I mean, nearly every
day a woman is killed.

00:08:08.101 --> 00:08:10.220
The levels of domestic violence

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:13.081
and how actually it's not investigated

00:08:13.081 --> 00:08:16.680
is something that it cannot
almost be stressed enough.

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:19.580
The country leaving
the Istanbul convention

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:21.610
earlier this year, no reason was given.

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:25.486
That was all about a
document signed in Istanbul,

00:08:25.486 --> 00:08:29.880
all about the prevention
of gender based violence.

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:31.640
What is your understanding,
just first of all,

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:36.120
of why Erdogan walked away from
that or his government did?

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:40.690
We have seen an escalation in
cases of femicides in Turkey

00:08:40.690 --> 00:08:44.860
and there are so many other
stories that go uninvestigated.

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:47.470
For instance, there is
suspicious suicides,

00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:49.520
are they really suicides?

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:52.400
So, this is an urgent issue

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:55.590
for women and children
and minorities in Turkey.

00:08:55.590 --> 00:08:59.320
But instead of doing something about it,

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:01.270
instead of changing the laws,

00:09:01.270 --> 00:09:03.610
and instead of implementing
Istanbul convention,

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:05.130
because signing it is not enough,

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:07.290
you need to ratify, you
need to implement it,

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:08.730
put it into practice.

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:10.105
So, instead of doing all that,

00:09:10.105 --> 00:09:13.260
what the government did
was the exact opposite.

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:17.110
They also launched this very
horrible smear campaign,

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:20.430
misinformation saying
that Istanbul convention

00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:23.600
is actually against
our traditional values.

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:26.350
And then one night, at night time,

00:09:26.350 --> 00:09:29.516
the government decided to withdraw

00:09:29.516 --> 00:09:33.090
from Istanbul convention
without really consulting women,

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:35.930
without really consulting
the civil society.

00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:36.763
And ever since,

00:09:36.763 --> 00:09:40.030
women have been bravely
on the streets protesting.

00:09:40.030 --> 00:09:43.610
There are many women in Turkey
who are incredibly strong

00:09:43.610 --> 00:09:46.910
and vocal and I have so
much respect for them.

00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:49.500
And when you look at
academia, media, medicine,

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:51.260
you will find them everywhere,

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:54.750
but except in one field where
there are very few women,

00:09:54.750 --> 00:09:58.600
especially as you go up the
echelons of decision making,

00:09:58.600 --> 00:09:59.960
and that's politics.

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:01.830
And that makes a huge difference.

00:10:01.830 --> 00:10:04.430
So, the ones who are making the decisions

00:10:04.430 --> 00:10:08.170
are usually very patriarchal,
conservative men.

00:10:08.170 --> 00:10:09.880
You used the word there femicide,

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:11.990
and actually not everybody uses it,

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:14.460
but nearly every day of
the year a woman is killed.

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:16.780
And that's only the
people who are recorded,

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:19.000
the women who are recorded in Turkey.

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:20.540
Do you think it's important as someone

00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:23.203
who thinks all the time
about what words to use,

00:10:23.203 --> 00:10:25.330
that that word is used

00:10:25.330 --> 00:10:27.490
and that it passes into common parlance?

00:10:27.490 --> 00:10:28.970
Yes. I think it's so important.

00:10:28.970 --> 00:10:31.320
We need to use the word femicide,

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:33.810
we need to use the word misogyny,

00:10:33.810 --> 00:10:35.160
but also we need to understand

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:39.530
that hate speech directed
against women is a crime

00:10:39.530 --> 00:10:41.670
and it leads to other crimes.

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:43.970
So, every case shows this.

00:10:43.970 --> 00:10:47.040
And I don't think I have
to mention social media

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:50.300
alongside the role of media,
in which, unfortunately,

00:10:50.300 --> 00:10:53.830
it's always women who are put
under the magnifying glass.

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:55.120
What was she wearing?

00:10:55.120 --> 00:10:57.690
What was she doing at
that hour of the night?

00:10:57.690 --> 00:10:58.860
Why was she out?

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:01.450
Immediately or why didn't
she defend herself?

00:11:01.450 --> 00:11:03.700
Immediately we start questioning women.

00:11:03.700 --> 00:11:06.310
This narrative has to change.

00:11:06.310 --> 00:11:08.450
And I think we also need to understand

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:10.080
that unfortunately with the pandemic,

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:12.310
we have seen an increase
in domestic violence

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:13.760
all across the world.

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:16.250
There was a recent report that showed,

00:11:16.250 --> 00:11:18.550
in over 40 countries,

00:11:18.550 --> 00:11:20.930
the amount of abuse
that women journalists,

00:11:20.930 --> 00:11:21.970
women politicians,

00:11:21.970 --> 00:11:23.850
any woman in the public space

00:11:23.850 --> 00:11:26.680
have been receiving has escalated.

00:11:26.680 --> 00:11:29.020
So, tech companies also need to wake up

00:11:29.020 --> 00:11:32.190
to the fact that this is hate
speech we're talking about,

00:11:32.190 --> 00:11:34.200
and they need to take it seriously.

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:35.210
With regards, of course,

00:11:35.210 --> 00:11:37.690
I mean, men receive an awful
amount of abuse as well,

00:11:37.690 --> 00:11:41.610
but it is augmented
differently towards women

00:11:41.610 --> 00:11:44.150
and for what reason it is given.

00:11:44.150 --> 00:11:47.440
And I suppose how you can tackle that.

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:49.710
I mean, some would say technology

00:11:49.710 --> 00:11:53.310
is just reflecting the ills
that's already there in society.

00:11:53.310 --> 00:11:55.740
And it's actually for society to change,

00:11:55.740 --> 00:11:57.530
as opposed to the platforms,

00:11:57.530 --> 00:12:00.240
but that has been a move
away from that position.

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:04.580
I think we need to talk
about tech companies

00:12:04.580 --> 00:12:07.610
in a different way, in
the sense that for me,

00:12:07.610 --> 00:12:09.080
particularly the digital platforms,

00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:10.900
social media is a bit like the moon.

00:12:10.900 --> 00:12:12.540
It definitely has a bright side.

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:14.380
Of course, it does connect us.

00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:16.450
It does give people more voice,

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:19.830
especially in countries where
there are no media freedoms

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:23.100
or in countries where
there's no equal space

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:25.850
in the urban public space,
for instance, for women.

00:12:25.850 --> 00:12:29.210
Many young women can carve
out a space for themselves

00:12:29.210 --> 00:12:30.670
in digital platforms.

00:12:30.670 --> 00:12:32.840
I do not belittle any of that.

00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:35.530
However, because we have over romanticized

00:12:35.530 --> 00:12:37.490
the bright side of technology,

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:40.930
and because we had this tech
optimism of early 2000s,

00:12:40.930 --> 00:12:43.210
which was so arrogant as well.

00:12:43.210 --> 00:12:46.660
Because the expectation
was if you bring Facebook

00:12:46.660 --> 00:12:48.030
and social media,

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:50.460
every country is going
to become a democracy.

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:51.780
That's not true.

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:52.990
Because of all of that,

00:12:52.990 --> 00:12:54.380
we urgently need to focus

00:12:54.380 --> 00:12:58.050
on the darker side of
social media platforms.

00:12:58.050 --> 00:13:01.220
And we need to understand how
they spread disinformation,

00:13:01.220 --> 00:13:03.513
misinformation, and hate speech.

00:13:04.589 --> 00:13:07.597
To come to, you know - I mean I would

00:13:07.597 --> 00:13:09.610
The technology companies would say

00:13:09.610 --> 00:13:10.950
they are working on that.

00:13:10.950 --> 00:13:12.510
That is something they're now looking at,

00:13:12.510 --> 00:13:13.720
or have been looking at.

00:13:13.720 --> 00:13:15.810
I mean, I was reading
about your early life.

00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:19.790
You said that books
saved you. Why was that?

00:13:19.790 --> 00:13:21.850
My parents were in France.

00:13:21.850 --> 00:13:22.940
I was born in France,

00:13:22.940 --> 00:13:24.910
but afterwards they got separated

00:13:24.910 --> 00:13:26.890
and my father stayed in France.

00:13:26.890 --> 00:13:29.080
And in the meantime, my
mother brought me to Ankara,

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:31.970
to Turkey, to a very
conservative neighborhood,

00:13:31.970 --> 00:13:33.380
very patriarchal neighborhood.

00:13:33.380 --> 00:13:35.430
This was my grandmother's house,

00:13:35.430 --> 00:13:37.120
but grandma herself was interesting.

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:38.930
She was a bit like a matriarch

00:13:38.930 --> 00:13:40.550
and people would come to her

00:13:40.550 --> 00:13:42.943
and her house was full of superstitions,

00:13:44.330 --> 00:13:48.770
lots of stories of the
Middle East and oral culture.

00:13:48.770 --> 00:13:51.090
Although, most of my upbringing

00:13:51.090 --> 00:13:53.210
and my education was in Turkey,

00:13:53.210 --> 00:13:56.300
I think I also felt the need
to start writing in English,

00:13:56.300 --> 00:13:58.750
even though, as you can hear in my accent,

00:13:58.750 --> 00:14:01.060
in my broken accent as well,

00:14:01.060 --> 00:14:02.510
I'm an immigrant in this language.

00:14:02.510 --> 00:14:04.710
English for me is an acquired language.

00:14:04.710 --> 00:14:06.890
It's a much more cerebral connection.

00:14:06.890 --> 00:14:09.800
My connection with the Turkish
language is more emotional,

00:14:09.800 --> 00:14:10.760
but writing in English

00:14:10.760 --> 00:14:14.590
gave me an additional sense
of freedom and I needed that.

00:14:14.590 --> 00:14:18.560
To this day, I realize if my
writing has more sorrow in it,

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:21.340
if it has more longing or melancholy,

00:14:21.340 --> 00:14:23.990
I find these things much
easier to express in Turkish.

00:14:23.990 --> 00:14:25.267
Really?

00:14:25.267 --> 00:14:26.630
But when it comes to humor, which I love,

00:14:26.630 --> 00:14:30.210
and irony and satire, I find
that much easier in English.

00:14:30.210 --> 00:14:31.510
What language do you dream in?

00:14:31.510 --> 00:14:32.738
That's always the test.

00:14:32.738 --> 00:14:34.060
Do you dream in both?

00:14:34.060 --> 00:14:35.070
I dream in both.

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:36.530
I dream a lot in English.

00:14:36.530 --> 00:14:38.560
When I'm writing in English, of course,

00:14:38.560 --> 00:14:40.580
I dream primarily, mostly in English.

00:14:40.580 --> 00:14:42.740
So, it can change?
Yeah, it can change.

00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:45.530
I love asking people who
speak a few languages,

00:14:45.530 --> 00:14:46.770
where they are with that,

00:14:46.770 --> 00:14:49.300
because it's a bit of a
moment when you switch over.

00:14:49.300 --> 00:14:50.240
That's so true.

00:14:50.240 --> 00:14:51.720
And the mind is fascinating, isn't it?

00:14:51.720 --> 00:14:54.410
Because it doesn't recognize
these national borders

00:14:54.410 --> 00:14:56.652
that we take for granted.

00:14:56.652 --> 00:14:58.030
You're not asked for your
passport in your sleep?

00:14:58.030 --> 00:14:58.863
Not at all.

00:14:58.863 --> 00:15:00.720
So, you can start the
same dream in Turkish

00:15:00.720 --> 00:15:02.540
and end it in English.

00:15:02.540 --> 00:15:06.195
And it also fascinates me
when I observe many immigrants

00:15:06.195 --> 00:15:09.130
or many families from complex backgrounds,

00:15:09.130 --> 00:15:12.700
sometimes they like to express
their anger or frustration

00:15:12.700 --> 00:15:15.700
in one language or another,
so that switch back and forth.

00:15:15.700 --> 00:15:17.140
It's so clever.

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:19.560
In the latest book, "The
Island of Missing Trees,"

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:21.900
why or what was the moment you thought

00:15:21.900 --> 00:15:25.380
I'd quite like to write from
the perspective of a fig tree?

00:15:25.380 --> 00:15:27.220
That was such a risk, to be honest.

00:15:27.220 --> 00:15:29.420
I was aware that it
was risk for an author,

00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:32.410
because if it doesn't work,
the whole thing collapses.

00:15:32.410 --> 00:15:34.440
The whole edifice collapses.

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:37.320
But it felt like the right thing to do.

00:15:37.320 --> 00:15:38.870
And if I may take a step back,

00:15:38.870 --> 00:15:40.780
I've been wanting to write about Cyprus

00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:41.810
for a very long time-

00:15:41.810 --> 00:15:42.670
Which is what the book-

00:15:42.670 --> 00:15:46.200
Which is where the book
partly takes place.

00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:48.590
And it's a beautiful island
with beautiful people,

00:15:48.590 --> 00:15:49.780
north and south.

00:15:49.780 --> 00:15:52.530
But at the same time, it's a
very difficult story to tell.

00:15:52.530 --> 00:15:55.420
And as you know, as we are
speaking, there's a border,

00:15:55.420 --> 00:15:57.790
there's a frontier, partition line

00:15:57.790 --> 00:16:00.360
that literally separates
Christians from Muslims,

00:16:00.360 --> 00:16:02.780
Greek Cypriots from Turkish Cypriots.

00:16:02.780 --> 00:16:05.600
So, depending on who you
talk to north or south,

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:07.270
there are clashing memories

00:16:07.270 --> 00:16:10.230
and there are wounds and
the wounds are unhealed.

00:16:10.230 --> 00:16:12.300
I couldn't find the gate into the story.

00:16:12.300 --> 00:16:15.000
I've been thinking, reading, researching.

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:17.710
Only when I found the
voice of the fig tree,

00:16:17.710 --> 00:16:20.230
it allows me to separate myself

00:16:20.230 --> 00:16:23.690
from these nationalistic
boxes and take another look.

00:16:23.690 --> 00:16:26.860
And then I felt more
free to tell the story.

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:28.650
But about what people don't say,

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:30.270
that's also a theme in the book.

00:16:30.270 --> 00:16:33.560
And it's about what you
don't say to your children,

00:16:33.560 --> 00:16:37.120
how you protect them,
potentially from painful pasts,

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:40.490
what you as a family have perhaps
fled from or escaped from.

00:16:40.490 --> 00:16:41.590
You have two children

00:16:41.590 --> 00:16:44.280
and I wonder how much
you've navigated that

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:45.250
with your own children,

00:16:45.250 --> 00:16:49.160
what you say about why you
really left when you left Turkey.

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:51.650
Having your mother being
heavily pregnant with you

00:16:51.650 --> 00:16:53.410
while being investigated in the way

00:16:53.410 --> 00:16:56.930
that you've described to
me today is extraordinary.

00:16:56.930 --> 00:16:59.490
Yes, these are not easy
conversations to have,

00:16:59.490 --> 00:17:01.010
I'm aware of that.

00:17:01.010 --> 00:17:03.620
At the same time, as a novelist,

00:17:03.620 --> 00:17:07.090
I also love to be an intellectual nomad

00:17:07.090 --> 00:17:08.470
to the best of my abilities.

00:17:08.470 --> 00:17:09.860
So, I need to look at the story

00:17:09.860 --> 00:17:13.590
from the perspective of
multiple generations.

00:17:13.590 --> 00:17:14.930
And the reason why I say this is,

00:17:14.930 --> 00:17:17.230
because I think in many immigrant families

00:17:17.230 --> 00:17:20.050
or any family that comes
from a complex background,

00:17:20.050 --> 00:17:22.670
there are these striking differences

00:17:22.670 --> 00:17:25.140
as you move from one
generation to the next.

00:17:25.140 --> 00:17:27.240
The first generation are usually the ones

00:17:27.240 --> 00:17:29.400
who have experienced
the biggest hardships,

00:17:29.400 --> 00:17:31.760
traumas, obstacles.

00:17:31.760 --> 00:17:33.730
And they carry those memories within,

00:17:33.730 --> 00:17:36.700
but they don't know how to
talk about them, so they don't.

00:17:36.700 --> 00:17:40.290
The second generation,
especially after displacement,

00:17:40.290 --> 00:17:42.570
they're not interested in the past.

00:17:42.570 --> 00:17:45.300
And understandably, they
have to be forward looking.

00:17:45.300 --> 00:17:47.310
They need to find their feet, build a-

00:17:47.310 --> 00:17:48.310
New life.

00:17:48.310 --> 00:17:51.500
Assimilate, tabula rasa,
let's move forward.

00:17:51.500 --> 00:17:54.310
And therefore the third
or the fourth generations

00:17:54.310 --> 00:17:55.220
in these families,

00:17:55.220 --> 00:17:57.140
the youngest in these families

00:17:57.140 --> 00:18:00.220
who are asking the strongest
questions about identity,

00:18:00.220 --> 00:18:02.760
memory, their ancestors' journeys.

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:04.310
And I find that very striking

00:18:04.310 --> 00:18:06.810
that sometimes you can meet young people

00:18:06.810 --> 00:18:08.440
carrying old memories.

00:18:08.440 --> 00:18:09.663
It's so true.

00:18:09.663 --> 00:18:12.110
Have you made a decision
then based on that

00:18:12.110 --> 00:18:14.130
to try and skip being the generation,

00:18:14.130 --> 00:18:16.760
because you have moved and changed

00:18:16.760 --> 00:18:18.980
that doesn't answer the
questions or comes forward.

00:18:18.980 --> 00:18:20.440
Are you trying to disrupt that?

00:18:20.440 --> 00:18:21.630
I want to disrupt that.

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:25.300
I want to understand those silences

00:18:25.300 --> 00:18:27.590
because I think silences keep us apart.

00:18:27.590 --> 00:18:31.200
And they also deepen and
widen the existing hurts

00:18:31.200 --> 00:18:32.620
and inequalities,

00:18:32.620 --> 00:18:34.560
but stories bring us together.

00:18:34.560 --> 00:18:36.925
If we understand each other's stories,

00:18:36.925 --> 00:18:41.925
then we can feel someone
else's psychology.

00:18:42.850 --> 00:18:46.414
I am someone who thinks
about a lot about emotions.

00:18:46.414 --> 00:18:48.500
And I think we're living
in an age of angst

00:18:48.500 --> 00:18:51.100
and it's not easy to be young in this age.

00:18:51.100 --> 00:18:52.850
It feels to me like many young people,

00:18:52.850 --> 00:18:55.770
they have almost a
scream building up inside

00:18:55.770 --> 00:18:58.390
and they want to unleash it at us

00:18:58.390 --> 00:19:00.220
because they think we're destroying.

00:19:00.220 --> 00:19:03.050
Rightly so, we're destroying
this world for them.

00:19:03.050 --> 00:19:05.010
It's going to impact their future.

00:19:05.010 --> 00:19:08.030
So, it's a moment of anxiety, fear,

00:19:08.030 --> 00:19:11.950
disappointment,
bewilderment, anger as well.

00:19:11.950 --> 00:19:14.670
But I think rather than
questioning these emotions,

00:19:14.670 --> 00:19:17.970
the real question is what do
we do with these emotions.

00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:19.360
What do we do with our anger?

00:19:19.360 --> 00:19:20.670
What do we do with our anxiety?

00:19:20.670 --> 00:19:23.030
Can we turn it into
something more positive?

00:19:23.030 --> 00:19:26.260
There's only one emotion
that really scares me

00:19:26.260 --> 00:19:28.690
and that's the lack of all emotions.

00:19:28.690 --> 00:19:31.910
That's numbness, the moment we
stop listening to each other,

00:19:31.910 --> 00:19:33.950
the moment we become indifferent.

00:19:33.950 --> 00:19:34.860
The indifference.

00:19:34.860 --> 00:19:37.150
That's I think a much
darker world to live in.

00:19:37.150 --> 00:19:39.430
That scream of anger has made me recall

00:19:39.430 --> 00:19:42.350
that I read you're a heavy metal fan.

00:19:42.350 --> 00:19:44.700
Is that how you release, Elif?

00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:46.666
I love heavy metal. I
always loved heavy metal.

00:19:46.666 --> 00:19:47.499
Of course, you do.

00:19:47.499 --> 00:19:49.490
That's exactly what I thought
when you walked in today.

00:19:49.490 --> 00:19:50.490
Do you really?

00:19:50.490 --> 00:19:51.705
I do.

00:19:51.705 --> 00:19:53.340
I do very much so, since my early youth.

00:19:53.340 --> 00:19:55.770
People don't expect a
middle aged Turkish woman

00:19:55.770 --> 00:19:57.930
to love heavy metal, but I do.

00:19:57.930 --> 00:19:58.900
Is that what you're listening to

00:19:58.900 --> 00:20:00.650
when you wrote us the victory?

00:20:00.650 --> 00:20:02.260
Yes, I do listen to heavy metal,

00:20:02.260 --> 00:20:05.340
particularly the sub genres
like industrial metal,

00:20:05.340 --> 00:20:09.670
gothic, more metal core,
Viking, Scandinavian.

00:20:09.670 --> 00:20:10.610
Viking's my favorite.

00:20:10.610 --> 00:20:13.533
I've literally got no idea
what you're talking about.

00:20:14.370 --> 00:20:17.700
You also penned the 40 rules of love.

00:20:17.700 --> 00:20:19.090
What's the most important one?

00:20:19.090 --> 00:20:21.600
I think they're all related.

00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:25.880
I think love is so powerful
and it transforms us

00:20:25.880 --> 00:20:28.590
and we need to allow
ourselves to be transformed.

00:20:28.590 --> 00:20:31.650
And I think one of the
rules mentioned in the book

00:20:31.650 --> 00:20:33.640
is about freedom.

00:20:33.640 --> 00:20:36.230
Where there's no freedom there's no love.

00:20:36.230 --> 00:20:38.820
So, love is not about possessiveness.

00:20:38.820 --> 00:20:41.690
My fiance, my partner, my girlfriend,

00:20:41.690 --> 00:20:43.600
the emphasis is always on my,

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:45.740
but it's not about ownership, love.

00:20:45.740 --> 00:20:47.420
Freedom and love makes me think

00:20:47.420 --> 00:20:49.890
of your TED Talk back in 2017,

00:20:49.890 --> 00:20:51.830
where just towards the end

00:20:51.830 --> 00:20:54.650
you said you've never been
brave enough to admit that you,

00:20:54.650 --> 00:20:55.870
I think you said it in the past tense,

00:20:55.870 --> 00:20:57.790
that you were bisexual.

00:20:57.790 --> 00:20:58.640
No, I said, I am.

00:20:58.640 --> 00:20:59.473
You say I am.

00:20:59.473 --> 00:21:02.340
But I never had the
courage to say it publicly.

00:21:02.340 --> 00:21:04.040
And I watched that talk

00:21:04.040 --> 00:21:05.080
and it is right towards the end

00:21:05.080 --> 00:21:06.890
and it's great to hear
it in its full context.

00:21:06.890 --> 00:21:09.810
But of course, what was taken
from that is you came out.

00:21:09.810 --> 00:21:10.643
Yeah, of course.

00:21:10.643 --> 00:21:14.830
And you had been scared of
the shame, the ridicule.

00:21:14.830 --> 00:21:16.293
How has it been since?

00:21:17.923 --> 00:21:21.040
Well, unfortunately
again, from my motherland,

00:21:21.040 --> 00:21:25.000
the level of shaming,
ridicule, hate speech,

00:21:25.000 --> 00:21:28.620
verbal abuse has been awful,

00:21:28.620 --> 00:21:30.580
but I was ready to weather that storm.

00:21:30.580 --> 00:21:33.980
And I wish I had the
courage to come out earlier.

00:21:33.980 --> 00:21:36.150
Even though it's very
visible in my writing,

00:21:36.150 --> 00:21:37.720
even though I've always been very vocal

00:21:37.720 --> 00:21:40.610
about my support for LGBTQ+ rights,

00:21:40.610 --> 00:21:44.550
I never had the courage to
say, also, this is my story.

00:21:44.550 --> 00:21:46.910
But about with regards to feedback

00:21:46.910 --> 00:21:50.930
or the comments coming
from listeners or readers

00:21:50.930 --> 00:21:52.250
from other parts of the world,

00:21:52.250 --> 00:21:54.220
it has been incredibly heartwarming.

00:21:54.220 --> 00:21:58.100
And also, from my motherland,
I need to add this,

00:21:58.100 --> 00:22:01.790
from families, particularly of children

00:22:01.790 --> 00:22:04.310
who are LGBTQ themselves

00:22:04.310 --> 00:22:06.750
or both young and older generations,

00:22:06.750 --> 00:22:10.260
they have been very heartwarming
and very moving letters,

00:22:10.260 --> 00:22:13.480
quietly whispering words of support.

00:22:13.480 --> 00:22:16.400
But I had to go through that.
A lot of abuse afterwards.

00:22:16.400 --> 00:22:17.233
It's interesting,

00:22:17.233 --> 00:22:18.830
I was looking at some of the feedback

00:22:18.830 --> 00:22:19.730
that was certainly public.

00:22:19.730 --> 00:22:22.020
And there's this type that you described

00:22:22.020 --> 00:22:23.150
in terms of the anti,

00:22:23.150 --> 00:22:25.700
but there was also a
sense of, as I believe,

00:22:25.700 --> 00:22:28.690
you're married to a man, why
did you need to say this?

00:22:28.690 --> 00:22:31.380
We're beyond being able
to be in a position

00:22:31.380 --> 00:22:33.030
where you have to come out anymore.

00:22:33.030 --> 00:22:35.170
But of course, different
parts of the world

00:22:35.170 --> 00:22:36.290
and different parts of society

00:22:36.290 --> 00:22:39.140
are at such different
stages of that, aren't they?

00:22:39.140 --> 00:22:41.550
And I wonder, do you still think today

00:22:41.550 --> 00:22:44.140
it's important to be
able to say those things

00:22:44.140 --> 00:22:46.440
or do you think we're
post that in some way?

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:50.300
I think it's always important
to share our stories.

00:22:50.300 --> 00:22:53.410
And one of the things
that happens to us is,

00:22:53.410 --> 00:22:56.520
when you live, when you
deal with authoritarianism,

00:22:56.520 --> 00:22:58.860
when you deal with any ideology

00:22:58.860 --> 00:23:02.240
or structure that divides
human beings into boxes

00:23:02.240 --> 00:23:03.610
and denies their freedoms,

00:23:03.610 --> 00:23:06.870
what happens is people feel very lonely.

00:23:06.870 --> 00:23:08.087
People feel like,

00:23:08.087 --> 00:23:10.480
"Am I the only one
who's feeling this way?"

00:23:10.480 --> 00:23:12.571
But when you hear someone else's story,

00:23:12.571 --> 00:23:14.580
then you realize you're not alone.

00:23:14.580 --> 00:23:16.230
And that is very important.

00:23:16.230 --> 00:23:21.190
There are amazing authors
from LGBTQ+ communities,

00:23:21.190 --> 00:23:22.800
authors, poet, speakers,

00:23:22.800 --> 00:23:25.620
who have given me amazing energy

00:23:25.620 --> 00:23:28.370
and motivation and inspiration.

00:23:28.370 --> 00:23:31.790
So, I felt like I had a responsibility

00:23:31.790 --> 00:23:34.570
to share this primarily to myself,

00:23:34.570 --> 00:23:38.640
but also to anyone who
might be feeling alone.

00:23:38.640 --> 00:23:40.980
Elif Shafak, thank you so
much for talking to me.

00:23:40.980 --> 00:23:41.813
Thank you so much.

00:23:41.813 --> 00:23:43.569
Really appreciate it. It was fascinating.

00:23:43.569 --> 00:23:45.267
Me too. Thank you.

00:23:45.267 --> 00:23:47.300
Thank you so much for being with us.

00:23:47.300 --> 00:23:51.233
And until we meet again, mask
up, stay safe and goodbye.

